# gh-1540 (source #1505): the recruit link went to ~50 people on 09-01 and at
# least one recipient's client mangled the path. 200-rewrite (not 301, so the
# address bar and any query string the recipient's client kept are preserved)
# every variant of the recruit link onto recruit.html itself -- cheap
# insurance against link-mangling by messaging/PWA clients. Netlify forwards
# the incoming query string on rewrites by default, so ?code= survives.
# recruit.html's own client-side router then reads ?code= and routes to
# partners.html -- that JS routing is unchanged by this rule set.
/recruit       /recruit.html   200
/recruit/      /recruit.html   200
/Recruit.html  /recruit.html   200
/recruit.htm   /recruit.html   200

# gh-1261: landing.html is an orphaned page -- nothing in the repo links to
# it. Kept on disk (not deleted) so any inbound links survive; force-flagged
# because Netlify serves a matching static file ahead of a redirect
# otherwise (see the /inspector-landing.html rule above for the same
# reasoning -- that one avoided the problem by deleting the file instead).
/landing.html / 301!

# gh-1540 REFUTED-REOPENED fix: netlify.toml now sets
# [build.processing.html] pretty_urls = false, which stops Netlify's
# built-in trailing-slash/case pre-normalization from 301-ing /recruit/ and
# /Recruit.html ahead of the explicit 200 rules above (that pre-normalization
# ran BEFORE this file was ever consulted). Disabling Pretty URLs also turns
# off its site-wide implicit /page -> page.html resolution, so this
# catch-all restores that behavior explicitly for every other extensionless
# path on the site. Placed last so every specific rule above (including the
# gh-1540 block) still wins on an exact match; a rewrite target that has no
# corresponding *.html file falls through to the normal 404, same as before.
# Blast-radius check performed before adding this (gh-1540 report): grepped
# every href="/..." and src="/..." across *.html/*.js repo-wide for
# extensionless internal paths -- the only hits were root "/" (already
# index.html by default, unaffected) and directory links "/blog/" and
# "/guides/" (served by blog/index.html and guides/index.html, directory-index
# serving, unaffected by Pretty URLs). No hardcoded extensionless
# window.location/location.href assignments were found in js/*.js either.
/*  /:splat.html  200
